   "stem": {
      "text": "A particle moves along the curve \\( - x y + y^{3} = 6 \\) so that its coordinates \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are differentiable functions of time \\( t \\). At the instant the particle is at the point \\( (1, 2) \\), \\( \\frac{dx}{dt} = -4 \\) units per second. Find \\( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\), in units per second, at that instant."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         -8,
         11
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Differentiate both sides of \\( - x y + y^{3} = 6 \\) with respect to \\( t \\), using the product rule on the term that contains both variables: \\( \\left(- y\\right)\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\left(- x + 3 y^{2}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dt} = 0 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            "y"
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At \\( (1, 2) \\) the coefficient of \\( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\) is \\( -2 \\) and the coefficient of \\( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\) is \\( 11 \\). Substitute \\( \\frac{dx}{dt} = -4 \\): \\( (-2)(-4) + (11)\\frac{dy}{dt} = 0 \\).",
         "mathjson": 8
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Solve for the requested rate: \\( \\frac{dy}{dt} = - \\frac{8}{11} \\).",
